A working set of shell scripts (create-vm.sh, scale-vm.sh, delete-vm.sh, backup-vm.sh) that reliably automate the VM lifecycle using only stable kubevirt.io/v1 APIs
Timestamped, compressed backup archives of VM configuration stored under ~/vm-automation/backups, with a working cron schedule to produce them automatically
Consistent, timestamped logs for every automation action under ~/vm-automation/logs, demonstrating traceable error handling
Troubleshooting
Issue 1: oc wait --for=condition=Ready vmi ... times out during VM creation or scaling. Diagnostic hint: run oc get events -n vm-automation --sort-by=.lastTimestamp and check whether the container disk image is still being pulled, or whether the node has insufficient CPU/memory to schedule the launcher pod.

Issue 2: The cron job never produces a new backup archive. Diagnostic hint: confirm the cron daemon is running (systemctl status crond), check that the wrapper script uses absolute paths (relative paths fail under cron's minimal environment), and inspect ~/vm-automation/logs/cron-backup.log for the actual error emitted by backup-vm.sh.

Conclusion
In this lab, students built a complete set of shell-based automation scripts for managing OpenShift Virtualization VMs, covering creation, scaling, deletion, and configuration backup, entirely through the oc CLI and the stable kubevirt.io/v1 API. The scripts demonstrate practical automation patterns including input validation, idempotency checks, structured logging, and graceful shutdown before destructive operations, all of which are directly applicable to production VM fleet management. By implementing backups as compressed YAML exports rather than relying on optional snapshot controllers, the lab also illustrates how to build automation that degrades gracefully across differently configured clusters. Students who want to extend this work should explore integrating these scripts with a real snapshot or CSI-based backup solution where available, adding Slack or email alerting on script failure, and converting the cron-based schedule into a Kubernetes CronJob running inside the cluster for centralized management.
